# Valgrind suppressions for `make memleak`.
#
# SCOPE DISCIPLINE — every entry must satisfy all three:
#   1. The named frame is THIRD-PARTY (Zig standard library). No agentsfleet
#      frame may ever appear here; our own code does not get suppressed.
#   2. The error class is NOT a leak. `Memcheck:Leak` must never be suppressed —
#      catching leaks is the entire point of the gate.
#   3. The entry names one specific syscall parameter, not a whole subsystem,
#      so an unrelated defect reached through the same frame still fails.
#
# Adding an entry weakens a gate and carries the same approval requirement as
# any other gate change. If an entry would need an agentsfleet frame, the answer
# is to fix our code instead.

# Zig 0.16's threaded IO backend (`std.Io.Threaded`) parks a worker on a futex
# word that lives in the *spawning* thread's stack frame. Memcheck tracks stack
# bounds per thread, so when the waiting thread hands that address to futex(2)
# it reads as "unaddressable, on another thread's stack, below its stack
# pointer" — Memcheck cannot see that the owning frame is still live and
# deliberately parked.
#
# Seen only in the boot->SIGTERM->drain lifecycle lane, where the daemon brings
# up its real worker pool, and only intermittently: it turns on which thread
# wins the shutdown race, which is why one commit passes and the next fails with
# no relevant change between them. The same signature appears on branches that
# never touched telemetry (hotfix/m139-otlp-crash-and-log-gate), so it tracks
# the stdlib backend rather than any one change of ours.
#
# This suppresses ONE syscall parameter in ONE stdlib frame. A leak, an invalid
# free, or any other Memcheck error in the same test still fails the gate.
#
# Remove this entry when the daemon moves off the 0.16 threaded IO backend, or
# when upstream stops parking on a cross-thread stack word.
{
   zig-std-io-threaded-cross-thread-futex-word
   Memcheck:Param
   futex(futex)
   fun:*Io.Threaded.Future.start*
}

# Same root cause reached through the worker parking path rather than the
# future's own start. Kept separate so each entry stays narrow enough to read,
# and so retiring one when upstream fixes it cannot silently widen the other.
{
   zig-std-io-threaded-worker-park-futex-word
   Memcheck:Param
   futex(futex)
   fun:*Io.Threaded*
   fun:*worker*
}
